s two central modals, which standard English does not allow."}}},{"id":"62662711-6c30-59fa-a5e7-d1380d3e7b54","topicSlug":"modals-ability","level":"A2","prompt":"Express ability: “The assistant can ____ the rowing boat without help.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairs"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Ability is expressed by **can + base verb**.","explanation":"**Can** takes base **repair**, even after a singular subject.","keywords":["present-ability"],"skill":"present-ability","contextKey":"modals-ability-50","fingerprint":"0ca15aed46027a000e7ab9bfdb0c53b75052885b1e59f636de94813c4fa80999","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Ability is expressed by **can + base verb**.","**Can** takes base **repair**, even after a singular subject.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Central modals do not allow third-person -s on the following verb.","B":"Can alone does not take an -ing form.","C":"Can takes a bare infinitive, without to.","D":"**Can** takes base **repair**, even after a singular subject."}}},{"id":"de525441-7373-56be-a65b-fdd3ee9b4e18","topicSlug":"modals-ability","level":"A2","prompt":"Ask about ability: “____ she repair the rowing boat?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Does can"},{"id":"B","text":"Cans"},{"id":"C","text":"Is can"},{"id":"D","text":"Can"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"A modal forms its own question by moving before the subject.","explanation":"**Can** moves before she; no do is added.","keywords":["modal-question"],"skill":"modal-question","contextKey":"modals-ability-50","fingerprint":"15b41509a47515bb29d889c57e25eaab2fbc7973c1a3e02d56f560c4cddd79fb","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A modal forms its own question by moving before the subject.","**Can** moves before she; no do is added.","Choose **Can**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Do-support is not added to a can question.","B":"Can does not take an -s ending.","C":"Be is not added before can.","D":"**Can** moves before she; no do is added."}}},{"id":"39388df5-a59b-5767-a34f-06b99f982264","topicSlug":"modals-ability","level":"A2","prompt":"Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to ____ the rowing boat despite the difficulty.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repairs"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"repaired"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"After **managed to**, the next verb returns to its base form.","explanation":"**Managed to repair** describes successful completion on that occasion.","keywords":["past-achievement"],"skill":"past-achievement","contextKey":"modals-ability-50","fingerprint":"79c0ee2dfc6a35bfe5f799ab99d9fd0ef9c979cc1c5dab7c71708c523c8cc8a2","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["After **managed to**, the next verb returns to its base form.","**Managed to repair** describes successful completion on that occasion.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Managed to repair** describes successful completion on that occasion.","B":"The infinitive after to has no -s ending.","C":"Managed in this meaning takes a to-infinitive, not to + -ing.","D":"The past marking is on managed, not on the infinitive."}}},{"id":"3533c291-e466-5ae7-a673-d821c8dcec7c","topicSlug":"modals-ability","level":"A2","prompt":"Express future ability with able: “Next month, they will ____ the rowing boat without help.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"be able to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"can repair"},{"id":"C","text":"are able to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"be able repair"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Can has no infinitive form; use **be able to** after will.","explanation":"After **will**, use **be able to repair**; this supplies future ability without stacking modals.","keywords":["future-ability"],"skill":"future-ability","contextKey":"modals-ability-50","fingerprint":"f8ed9900e09e3af98f4cacf6849c66588b2a181a0eeed7a41a438493bea2e969","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Can has no infinitive form; use **be able to** after will.","After **will**, use **be able to repair**; this supplies future ability without stacking modals.","Choose **be able to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After **will**, use **be able to repair**; this supplies future ability without stacking modals.","B":"Will can combines two central modals, which standard English does not allow.","C":"After will, be must stay in its base form.","D":"Able requires to before the infinitive."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Use basic modals for ability and permission, and distinguish general past ability from a successful event.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"A modal keeps the next verb simple","explanation":"Can and could take a **base verb without to**. They do not take third-person -s. Can commonly describes present ability or informal permission. The surrounding context distinguishes those meanings.","pattern":"She **can swim**. / You **can leave** now.","commonMistakes":["She cans swim and she can to swim are not standard modal constructions."],"examples":["Express ability: “The assistant can **prepare** the visitor guide without help.”","Express ability: “The assistant can **restore** the old portrait without help.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explanation":"Move the modal before the subject in a question. Put not after it for a negative. Cannot is normally written as one word; can’t is its common contraction. No do or does is needed.","pattern":"**Can** they help? / They **cannot help**.","commonMistakes":["Do they can help? adds an unnecessary auxiliary."],"examples":["Ask about ability: “**Can** she prepare the visitor guide?”","Ask about ability: “**Can** she restore the old portrait?”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"General past ability","explanation":"Could can describe a general ability in the past. For one successful achievement on a particular occasion, affirmative was/were able to or managed to is often more natural. Could is still possible for perception and some mental processes.","pattern":"As a child, she **could swim**. / Yesterday, she **managed to escape**.","commonMistakes":["Could not can describe failure on one occasion; the restriction mainly concerns affirmative achievement."],"examples":["Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to **prepare** the visitor guide despite the difficulty.”","Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to **restore** the old portrait despite the difficulty.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Permission and polite requests","explanation":"Can I asks permission; could I is often more tentative. Could you commonly makes a polite request, not a claim about past ability. Be able to supplies forms unavailable to can, such as will be able to.","pattern":"**Could you help** me? / I’ll **be able to come**.","commonMistakes":["Do not stack two central modals: will can is not standard. Use will be able to."],"examples":["Express future ability with able: “Next month, they will **be able to prepare** the visitor guide without help.”","Express future ability with able: “Next month, they will **be able to restore** the old portrait without help.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["She **can swim**. / You **can leave** now.","**Can** they help? / They **cannot help**.","As a child, she **could swim**. / Yesterday, she **managed to escape**.","**Could you help** me? / I’ll **be able to come**."]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["adverbs-position"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
